{"step_index":174,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-08T21:19:19Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-09T05:19:19+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-09T05:19:19+08:00\nShowing lines 100-160 of 423\n100:   <div id=\"time-box\" style=\"margin-left:14px;margin-bottom:12px\">\n101:     <label style=\"font-size:0.9rem\">Seconds per Question\n102:       <input id=\"time-val\" type=\"number\" min=\"5\" max=\"3600\" value=\"20\" style=\"max-width:140px;margin-bottom:6px\">\n103:     </label>\n104:   </div>\n105: \n106:   <div style=\"text-align:right\">\n107:     <button id=\"close-opt-btn\" class=\"sec\" type=\"button\">Close Options</button>\n108:   </div>\n109: </div>\n110: \n111: <div id=\"questions-container\"></div>\n112: \n113: <div class=\"floating-add\">\n114:   <button id=\"add-q-btn\" class=\"plus-btn\" type=\"button\">+ Add Question</button>\n115: </div>\n116: \n117: </div>\n118: \n119: <script src=\"https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/10.14.0/firebase-app-compat.js\"></script>\n120: <script src=\"https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/10.14.0/firebase-firestore-compat.js\"></script>\n121: <script>\n122: const firebaseConfig = {\n123:   apiKey: \"AIzaSyBkqJ9WvtXfGJt19XK7SLK9v5cJwp4EVII\",\n124:   authDomain: \"sir-jasper-quiz.firebaseapp.com\",\n125:   projectId: \"sir-jasper-quiz\",\n126:   storageBucket: \"sir-jasper-quiz.firebasestorage.app\",\n127:   messagingSenderId: \"932129654171\",\n128:   appId: \"1:932129654171:web:793aa17dd1f130a62f4d3e\"\n129: };\n130: \n131: const defaultQuiz = {\n132:   title: \"C Quiz: Switch Case\",\n133:   description: \"20 items • 20 seconds per question • Do not switch tabs or windows.\",\n134:   settings: {\n135:     timePerQuestion: 20,\n136:     enableTimer: true,\n137:     enableTabViolation: true,\n138:     tabViolationPenalty: 1,\n139:     randomizeQuestions: true,\n140:     randomizeAnswers: true\n141:   },\n142:   questions: [\n143:     { q: \"Which C statement selects one of many code blocks to run based on the value of an expression?\", correct: \"switch\", wrongs: [\"for\", \"while\", \"goto\"], explanation: \"switch compares one expression against a list of constant case labels and runs the matching block.\" },\n144:     { q: \"Which type of expression can be used as the controlling expression of a switch in C?\", correct: \"int or char\", wrongs: [\"float\", \"double\", \"string (char*)\"], explanation: \"switch works with integral types such as int and char, not floating-point values or strings.\" },\n145:     { q: \"Which keyword prevents execution from falling through to the next case?\", correct: \"break\", wrongs: [\"continue\", \"return 0\", \"exit\"], explanation: \"break leaves the switch block immediately after the matching case finishes.\" },\n146:     { q: \"When does the default label run?\", correct: \"When no case label matches\", wrongs: [\"Always, before the cases\", \"Only when the first case fails\", \"Only if break is missing\"], explanation: \"default is the fallback block used when no case matches the expression.\" },\n147:     { q: \"What is the output?\\nint x = 2;\\nswitch (x) {\\n  case 1: printf(\\\"A\\\");\\n  case 2: printf(\\\"B\\\");\\n  case 3: printf(\\\"C\\\");\\n}\", correct: \"BC\", wrongs: [\"B\", \"C\", \"ABC\"], explanation: \"No break statements, so execution starts at case 2 and falls through to case 3.\" },\n148:     { q: \"What is the output?\\nint x = 3;\\nswitch (x) {\\n  case 1: printf(\\\"1\\\"); break;\\n  default: printf(\\\"D\\\");\\n}\", correct: \"D\", wrongs: [\"1\", \"1D\", \"Nothing is printed\"], explanation: \"x is 3, no case matches, so default runs.\" },\n149:     { q: \"Is the default label required in a switch statement?\", correct: \"No, it is optional\", wrongs: [\"Yes, always\", \"Yes, but only with char\", \"Only if there is no break\"], explanation: \"default is optional; without it, nothing runs when no case matches.\" },\n150:     { q: \"Which case label is valid in C?\", correct: \"case 'A':\", wrongs: [\"case x:  (x is a variable)\", \"case 1.5:\", \"case \\\"A\\\":\"], explanation: \"Case labels must be integer or character constants. Variables, floats and strings are not allowed.\" },\n151:     { q: \"What is the output?\\nchar c = 'b';\\nswitch (c) {\\n  case 'a': printf(\\\"1\\\"); break;\\n  case 'b': printf(\\\"2\\\");\\n  case 'c': printf(\\\"3\\\"); break;\\n}\", correct: \"23\", wrongs: [\"2\", \"3\", \"123\"], explanation: \"case 'b' matches and prints 2, has no break, then falls into case 'c' and prints 3 before break.\" },\n152:     { q: \"What happens if two case labels in the same switch have the same constant value?\", correct: \"Compile-time error\", wrongs: [\"The first one runs\", \"The last one runs\", \"Both run\"], explanation: \"Duplicate case values are not allowed and the compiler reports an error.\" },\n153:     { q: \"Where can the default label be placed inside a switch?\", correct: \"Anywhere inside the switch\", wrongs: [\"Only at the end\", \"Only at the beginning\", \"Only after a break\"], explanation: \"default may appear in any position; it is used only when no case matches.\" },\n154:     { q: \"What is the output when x = 1?\\nswitch (x) {\\n  case 1:\\n  case 2: printf(\\\"Low\\\"); break;\\n  case 3: printf(\\\"High\\\");\\n}\", correct: \"Low\", wrongs: [\"High\", \"LowHigh\", \"Nothing is printed\"], explanation: \"Stacked labels share one block, so case 1 falls into case 2's code, which prints Low and breaks.\" },\n155:     { q: \"Which is NOT allowed as the controlling expression of a switch in C?\", correct: \"A float variable, e.g. switch(f)\", wrongs: [\"An int variable, e.g. switch(x)\", \"A char variable, e.g. switch(c)\", \"An expression, e.g. switch(x % 3)\"], explanation: \"The controlling expression must be an integer type; float values are not allowed.\" },\n156:     { q: \"Which statement about case labels is true?\", correct: \"They must be constant expressions\", wrongs: [\"They can be any variable\", \"They can be float values\", \"They can be strings\"], explanation: \"The compiler needs constant integer or character values to build the jump table.\" },\n157:     { q: \"What is the output?\\nint x = 5;\\nswitch (x % 2) {\\n  case 0: printf(\\\"Even\\\"); break;\\n  case 1: printf(\\\"Odd\\\"); break;\\n}\", correct: \"Odd\", wrongs: [\"Even\", \"EvenOdd\", \"Nothing is printed\"], explanation: \"5 % 2 is 1, so case 1 runs.\" },\n158:     { q: \"What is the output?\\nint x = 1;\\nswitch (x) {\\n  default: printf(\\\"D\\\");\\n  case 1: printf(\\\"1\\\"); break;\\n  case 2: printf(\\\"2\\\");\\n}\", correct: \"1\", wrongs: [\"D1\", \"D\", \"12\"], explanation: \"x matches case 1 directly, so default is skipped. Only 1 is printed, then break.\" },\n159:     { q: \"When is switch preferred over an if-else if chain?\", correct: \"Comparing one variable against several constant values\", wrongs: [\"Testing ranges like x > 10\", \"Comparing strings\", \"Evaluating floating-point conditions\"], explanation: \"switch is designed for equality checks of one integral value against constants. It cannot test ranges.\" },\n160:     { q: \"What is the value printed?\\nint a = 1;\\nswitch (a) {\\n  case 1: a++;\\n  case 2: a += 2; break;\\n  case 3: a = 10;\\n}\\nprintf(\\\"%d\\\", a);\", correct: \"4\", wrongs: [\"3\", \"2\", \"10\"], explanation: \"case 1 makes a = 2, falls into case 2 so a = 4, then break.\" },"}
